# Step 08 — Agent Registry Backend Implementation

## 1. Objective

Implement the finalized 29-agent master registry in the existing GeFlow backend.

The registry will become the authoritative backend source for identifying, configuring, mapping, validating, retrieving and managing GeFlow's specialized business-category agents.

This implementation must follow the previously finalized documents:

1. Step 04 — 29-Agent Master Registry Finalization.
2. Step 05 — Category-to-Agent Mapping Definition.
3. Step 06 — Agent Contract & Runtime Specification Finalization.
4. Step 07 — Organization-Level & Category-Level Authorization Implementation.

**Primary objective:** Convert the approved registry specification into a working, persistent, validated backend system without prematurely implementing agent execution, AI orchestration or agent-to-agent communication.

---

## 2. Scope and implementation boundaries

### Included in this step

- Inspect the existing backend and database architecture.
- Implement persistent agent registry storage.
- Register all 29 finalized agents.
- Implement category-to-agent registry associations.
- Implement registry validation and integrity constraints.
- Implement backend services for registry operations.
- Implement authorized registry retrieval and management endpoints, where the existing API architecture supports them.
- Integrate organization/category authorization from Step 07, after verifying its actual implementation.
- Implement agent status and configuration management.
- Implement registry versioning and audit logging where supported by the existing architecture.
- Add automated tests and regression tests.
- Document actual implementation and outstanding limitations.

### Explicitly excluded

Do not implement any of the following in this step:

- AI agent runtime or execution engine.
- Agent reasoning, task execution or autonomous operations.
- AI model integrations or provider selection.
- Agent-to-agent communication or delegation.
- Central AI orchestrator.
- Agent memory or conversation storage.
- Category-specific business workflows.
- New POS, inventory, purchasing or reporting features.
- Agent dashboard or registry management UI.
- Billing, subscription or pricing restrictions.
- New authentication or authorization framework.
- Deployment of 29 separate AI models or servers.

These are separate concerns and must not be bundled into this implementation.

