## 4. Registry architecture

Implement a centralized, persistent registry with logical separation between:

1. Agent identity.
2. Agent configuration.
3. Category association.
4. Agent lifecycle status.
5. Registry version information.
6. Organization-specific agent access, if required by the existing authorization model.

The architecture must support all 29 specialized agents through a common registry contract.

### 4.1 Core architecture

Conceptual architecture:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[GeFlow Backend] --> B[Agent Registry Service]
    B --> C[Registry Validation]
    C --> D[Agent Registry Storage]
    B --> E[Category Mapping Service]
    E --> F[Existing Category Data]
    B --> G[Authorization Layer]
    G --> H[Organization and Category Permissions]
    B --> I[Registry API]
    I --> J[Authorized Backend Consumers]
```

This is an architectural representation, not a request to create an entirely new backend framework.

Reuse existing application architecture wherever possible.

