## 5. Agent registry data model

Implement the registry using the database conventions and naming standards already present in GeFlow.

The following is the logical data model. Actual table names, field types and relationships must follow the inspected database architecture.

### 5.1 Core agent registry

Each agent must have a persistent registry record.

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| id | Internal primary identifier |
| agent_id | Permanent identifier, e.g. AGT-001 |
| name | Official agent name |
| description | Agent's functional purpose |
| agent_type | Specialized business-category agent |
| primary_category_id | Associated primary category |
| status | Current lifecycle status |
| version | Agent configuration version |
| contract_version | Compatible agent contract version |
| configuration | Validated agent configuration |
| capabilities | Declared capability identifiers |
| permissions | Declared permission references |
| created_at | Creation timestamp |
| updated_at | Last update timestamp |
| created_by | Creating user/system reference |
| updated_by | Last updating user/system reference |

Requirements:

- `agent_id` must be unique.
- IDs must remain stable across configuration updates.
- All 29 finalized agents must be represented.
- Category references must resolve against actual category records or the established category identifier system.
- Configuration must be validated before persistence.
- Sensitive credentials, API keys and secrets must never be stored in ordinary agent configuration fields.
- Do not store organization-specific private context in global registry records.

If the actual application uses JSONB, document storage or another configuration mechanism, follow the existing database design.

### 5.2 Category-to-agent association

Implement the association between GeFlow categories and their primary agents.

Required logical fields:

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| id | Association identifier |
| category_id | Existing GeFlow category identifier |
| agent_id | Associated agent identifier |
| is_primary | Whether this is the primary agent |
| mapping_version | Mapping version |
| status | Active or inactive association |
| created_at | Creation timestamp |
| updated_at | Last update timestamp |

Integrity requirements:

- Every finalized category must have its correct primary agent.
- Every agent must have its correct primary category.
- The initial registry must preserve the approved one-to-one mapping.
- Prevent duplicate primary mappings for the same category.
- Prevent associations with nonexistent categories or agents.
- Do not invent new category IDs if actual category IDs already exist.

The initial implementation must contain exactly 29 finalized primary associations.

Do not add secondary agents or multi-agent category routing in this step.

### 5.3 Agent configuration structure

Each agent must have its own configuration record or equivalent configuration object.

The configuration should support:

- Agent identity.
- Agent description.
- Specialized category.
- Responsibilities.
- Operational boundaries.
- Declared capabilities.
- Allowed permission references.
- Supported business module references.
- Compatible contract version.
- Configuration version.
- Lifecycle status.

Example conceptual configuration:

```json
{
  "agent_id": "AGT-001",
  "name": "General Retail Agent",
  "agent_type": "category_specialized",
  "primary_category_id": "CAT-001",
  "status": "registered",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "contract_version": "1.0",
  "capabilities": [],
  "permissions": [],
  "configuration": {
    "responsibilities": [],
    "boundaries": [],
    "supported_modules": []
  }
}
```

This JSON is an illustrative structure, not an instruction to insert empty capabilities or invent unapproved business functionality.

Populate the actual agent configurations using the finalized Step 04–06 specifications.

