## 7. Registry lifecycle management

Implement the registry lifecycle as a backend-managed state.

Suggested logical states:

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| registered | Agent identity exists in the registry |
| active | Agent configuration is approved and available to authorized consumers |
| inactive | Agent is intentionally unavailable |
| deprecated | Agent is being phased out |
| archived | Agent is retained for historical reference |

Use existing project conventions if equivalent statuses already exist.

### Lifecycle restrictions

- A newly registered agent must not automatically become executable.
- Registration does not imply AI runtime deployment.
- An inactive agent must not be returned as an active agent.
- A deprecated agent must not be assigned to new category mappings without explicit approval.
- Archived agents must remain traceable where historical records reference them.
- Status changes must be validated and authorized.
- Invalid lifecycle transitions must be rejected.

If the current architecture does not support a safe lifecycle transition, document the limitation instead of introducing an unnecessarily complex state machine.

