## 9. Registry API integration

Inspect the current API architecture before choosing routes.

If the project already has an API versioning and routing convention, follow it.

Possible logical endpoints:

| Method | Logical endpoint | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/agents` | Retrieve authorized registry entries |
| GET | `/agents/:agentId` | Retrieve one agent |
| GET | `/agents/category/:categoryId` | Retrieve category's primary agent |
| GET | `/agents/:agentId/configuration` | Retrieve authorized configuration |
| POST | `/agents` | Register an agent through an authorized management operation |
| PATCH | `/agents/:agentId` | Update permitted metadata |
| PATCH | `/agents/:agentId/status` | Change lifecycle status |
| PATCH | `/agents/:agentId/configuration` | Update configuration |
| GET | `/agents/:agentId/history` | Retrieve authorized registry history |

These are proposed logical routes, not mandatory literal paths.

Do not create duplicate API versions or competing route structures.

### API requirements

- Validate every request server-side.
- Validate request body and path parameters.
- Enforce authentication and authorization.
- Apply consistent error responses.
- Prevent mass assignment of protected fields.
- Do not expose internal secrets or sensitive infrastructure data.
- Apply pagination to registry listing if consistent with existing API conventions.
- Do not expose unrestricted administrative operations to ordinary users.

If the project uses server actions or direct database access rather than conventional REST APIs, implement equivalent backend operations in the established architecture instead.

